t i felt that a sci-fi film doesn't  often have  um but when you can put that into a  genre film i find that those are the  best  types of genre films is when they have  that gravity to them where you know you  can have all this frivolity in a lot of  ways and expansion but at the heart of  it you have um you know  a real story  when you read a script do you go into it  with an eye on the character you'd be  playing to see what you'd be doing or do  you try to read it more for the overall  story the first time you'd experience it  it's definitely kind of a a two-sided  thing you know it's definitely you know  uh on one hand you know what is this  character like what would it be like to  become this character what would it feel  like to step into her shoes  um  you know what you know does it scare me  as an actor you know does it does it  kind of get your get your kind of uh you  know uh fire underneath you lit to want  to have that drive to sink your teeth  into it and then on the other hand you  obviously want to watch it um you want  to you know read it as if you're  watching it and see  you know  how much of it you know lends itself to  viewership you know is it interesting is  it exciting is it enticing um does it  flow you know how much work might need  to be done to the script when you talk  to the director and all those type  things and honestly it  it was perfect as is and they were just  little things that i wanted to find with  algae in the conversation pieces that  felt right for the character and to make  her not this glowing  matriarchal  you know pregnancies amazing kind of  female  but more like this is [ __ ] hard you  know this is difficult and do we want to  do this how are we doing this you know  and can they rise above it  your primary co-star in this film is the  pregnant belly so  what was that like to wear was it  different than you expected what was  that like  yeah it was  so difficult you know it was a 21 pound  silicone belly attached to a